-- 1180: Blog post - "Hello World, Then a Robot: Our 8 August 2017 Arduino Electronics Class"
--
--       First blog post for WSQ - Practical Electronics Design with Arduino
--       Microcontroller (TGS-2020506075). Covers the 8 Aug 2017 class from the
--       course album. Cross-links to the 7 Apr 2017 IoT class post (1176), the
--       nearest sibling by date and subject.
--       Ships already published (status 1); linkedin_urn carries the manual-skip
--       sentinel (back-dated post - standing rule, see the blog-pipeline skill).
--       published_at pinned to 2017-08-08.
--
-- PHOTOGRAPHS: five frames from the class album, all 1008px-native Facebook
-- exports at 78-137KB - every one ALREADY under the <150KB target. A 900px q62
-- re-encode came out LARGER on all five (98-157KB), and q50 only saved ~15KB
-- while adding a generation of loss, so the originals were uploaded AS-IS.
-- All five verified HTTP 200 under blog/arduino/2017-08-08-arduino-*.jpg.
--   class-photo     - room shot, the class in session
--   hands-on-pair   - two learners wiring boards
--   lcd-hello-world - Arduino Mega + 16x2 LCD reading "Hello World!"
--   wiring-closeup  - two benches mid-build
--   ultrasonic-rig  - HC-SR04 + LCD reading 237 cm
--
-- Every course figure was read off the LIVE course page on 2026-08-29: $400
-- before GST, GST $36 (9%), nett $236 (SG/PR 21+, 50%) / $156 (SG 40+ or SME,
-- 70%), 1 day / 8 hrs + 1 hr assessment at Woods Square Tower 1 (Woodlands),
-- Beginner level, TSC Embedded Systems Integration ICT-SYS-3001-1.1-1, funding
-- valid 1 Nov 2020 - 31 Oct 2026, Arduino kit used in training but NOT included
-- in the fee, Arduino IDE on Windows/Mac.
--
-- FUNDING HONESTY: WSQ funding for this course began 1 Nov 2020, so the copy
-- says plainly that the 2017 class carried no funding - today's funded fees are
-- contrasted, not retro-fitted.
--
-- NO TRAINER NAMED: the album carries no trainer attribution, so none is
-- claimed. No biography or credential is asserted about anyone in the photos.
--
-- Notes for future editors:
--  * apply.php splits statements on ";" at end-of-line - the content HTML is ONE line.
--  * Content is pure ASCII; excerpt uses a REAL em-dash via CHAR() (see 1161);
--    meta_description carries NO em-dash entity (see 1164).
--  * FAQ ships as native <details> accordions per the blog-pipeline rules.
--  * Idempotent: WHERE NOT EXISTS on url_key, INSERT IGNORE on tag links.
--  * SG-only guard via @mms_instance.
--  * hero_image_url is NOT set here - generated on prod post-deploy (kicker "Arduino").

SET @is_sg := IF(@mms_instance = 'SG', 1, 0);


INSERT INTO `mmd_blog_post` (`title`, `url_key`, `excerpt`, `content`, `author`, `status`, `published_at`, `related_skus`, `source_sku`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `meta_keywords`, `linkedin_urn`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) SELECT 'Hello World, Then a Robot: Our 8 August 2017 Arduino Electronics Class', 'arduino-electronics-class-august-2017', CONCAT('On 8 August 2017 a room of beginners spent one day turning code into light, movement and a live distance reading. A look back at our Practical Electronics Design with Arduino class', CHAR(226,128,148 USING utf8), ' and what the same day costs now that it carries WSQ funding.'), '<p>On <strong>8 August 2017</strong> a small group sat down in our training room with a laptop each, a breadboard, and a handful of components that cost less than lunch. By the end of the day every one of them had written code that made something in the physical world move, light up, or measure a distance. That is the whole promise of the Arduino microcontroller, and it is why this course has stayed on our schedule for nearly a decade.</p> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"><img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/arduino/2017-08-08-arduino-class-photo.jpg" alt="Arduino electronics class in progress at Tertiary Infotech Academy, 8 August 2017" loading="lazy" style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:8px" /><figcaption>Our 8 August 2017 Practical Electronics Design with Arduino class. Laptops, breadboards and a room learning embedded systems from scratch.</figcaption></figure> <p>The class ran as a single full day &mdash; the same shape it runs in today. No prior electronics background was assumed. Most people in the room had never stripped a wire or read a resistor colour code before that morning.</p> <h2>Starting where every embedded engineer starts</h2> <p>The first hour is deliberately unglamorous: what a microcontroller actually is, how it differs from the computer on your desk, and how to get a single line of text out of the board over the serial port. It sounds trivial. It is not. Serial output is the debugging lifeline for every embedded project that follows, and learners who skip it spend the rest of their careers guessing why a circuit is silent.</p> <p>From there the day moves to digital input and output, and to Pulse Width Modulation &mdash; the trick that lets a chip which can only say <em>on</em> or <em>off</em> convince an LED to fade or a motor to run at half speed.</p> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"><img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/arduino/2017-08-08-arduino-hands-on-pair.jpg" alt="Two learners wiring Arduino boards and breadboards during the August 2017 class" loading="lazy" style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:8px" /><figcaption>Hands-on from the first hour. Every learner builds their own circuits rather than watching a demonstration.</figcaption></figure> <p>What the photograph above does not show is the amount of quiet troubleshooting that fills a hands-on day. A jumper in the wrong row. A component seated across the breadboard''s centre channel instead of straddling it. A sketch uploaded to the wrong serial port. Each one is a five-minute fix and a lesson that sticks far better than a slide would.</p> <h2>Getting a display to speak</h2> <p>The first real milestone of the day is the 16x2 character LCD. Wiring one up means dealing with a data bus, a contrast potentiometer, and a backlight &mdash; and getting all of it right before a single character appears. When it does, it says the two words every programmer has earned at least once.</p> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"><img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/arduino/2017-08-08-arduino-lcd-hello-world.jpg" alt="Arduino Mega wired to a 16x2 LCD displaying Hello World" loading="lazy" style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:8px" /><figcaption>The first milestone: an Arduino Mega driving a 16x2 character LCD. Getting "Hello World!" onto that screen means the data bus, contrast and backlight are all wired correctly.</figcaption></figure> <p>That blue glow is worth more to a beginner than an hour of theory. It is the moment the abstraction closes: code on a screen became voltage on a pin became light in the room.</p> <h2>Sensors, and the jump to something useful</h2> <p>Once output works, the course turns the direction around. Topic 3 covers analogue sensors and transducers &mdash; the continuously varying signals that a potentiometer, a light-dependent resistor or a temperature sensor produce, and how the board''s analogue-to-digital converter turns them into numbers a program can reason about. Servo control lands in the same topic, because a servo is the clearest possible demonstration that a number in a variable can become an angle in the world.</p> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"><img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/arduino/2017-08-08-arduino-wiring-closeup.jpg" alt="Close-up of Arduino boards wired to sensors and a breadboard on the classroom bench" loading="lazy" style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:8px" /><figcaption>Two boards, two builds. Learners work in pairs but each wires their own circuit.</figcaption></figure> <p>Topic 4 moves to digital sensors, including motion detection driving a light &mdash; the same pattern behind every corridor sensor in every office building in Singapore. Topic 5 closes the loop with actuators and stepper motor control, which is where robotics genuinely begins: precise, repeatable movement under program control.</p> <h2>The build that ties it together</h2> <p>The rig below is where the separate pieces stop being separate. An ultrasonic sensor measures the distance to whatever is in front of it, the microcontroller converts the echo time into centimetres, and the LCD reports the result live &mdash; here reading 237 cm. Sense, compute, display. Add a motor and you have a robot that stops before it hits a wall.</p> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"><img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/arduino/2017-08-08-arduino-ultrasonic-rig.jpg" alt="Arduino ultrasonic distance sensor rig displaying a 237 cm reading on an LCD" loading="lazy" style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:8px" /><figcaption>The payoff at the end of the day: an ultrasonic sensor, an Arduino and an LCD reading 237 cm live. Sense, compute, display &mdash; the pattern behind every robot that avoids a wall.</figcaption></figure> <p>Look closely at the wiring and you can see why we insist on a full hands-on day rather than a demonstration. Roughly thirty connections have to be simultaneously correct for that number to appear. Learning to build that carefully &mdash; and to find the one wire that is wrong &mdash; is the actual skill.</p> <h2>What the 2017 class did not have</h2> <p>One honest note. In August 2017 this course carried <strong>no WSQ funding</strong>; every learner in that room paid the full fee. Funding for the course became available on <strong>1 November 2020</strong> and is currently valid through <strong>31 October 2026</strong>. The same one-day class today is accredited against the Skills Framework TSC <em>Embedded Systems Integration</em> (ICT-SYS-3001-1.1-1), and the fees look very different:</p> <ul> <li>Full fee <strong>$400</strong> before GST (GST $36 at 9%).</li> <li><strong>$236 nett</strong> for Singapore Citizens and PRs aged 21 and above &mdash; 50% funded.</li> <li><strong>$156 nett</strong> for Singapore Citizens aged 40 and above, or SME-sponsored staff &mdash; 70% funded.</li> </ul> <p>Eligible learners can further offset the payable amount with SkillsFuture Credit; NTUC members may claim UTAP, and companies can tap SkillsFuture Enterprise Credit and Absentee Payroll. Passing the assessment and meeting 75% attendance earns both a Certificate of Achievement and an OpenCert Statement of Achievement from SkillsFuture Singapore &mdash; neither of which existed for this course in 2017.</p> <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2> <div class="mmd-faq"> <details class="mmd-faq-item"><summary class="mmd-faq-q">Do I need an electronics or programming background?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>No. The course is pitched at Beginner level. You need basic computer literacy and the ability to operate a laptop. The 2017 class was mostly first-timers, and that is still the intended audience.</p></div></details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"><summary class="mmd-faq-q">How long is the course?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>One day &mdash; 8 hours of instruction plus a 1-hour assessment, running 9:30am to 6:30pm. There is also an evening option spread over two evenings, 6:00pm to 10:00pm.</p></div></details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"><summary class="mmd-faq-q">Is the Arduino kit included in the fee?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>No. The kit is used during the training but is not included in the course fee. Bring your own laptop for the hands-on work; a spare can be provided if you do not have one.</p></div></details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"><summary class="mmd-faq-q">What software do I need to install?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>The Arduino IDE, which is free and runs on both Windows and Mac laptops.</p></div></details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"><summary class="mmd-faq-q">Was the 2017 class WSQ funded?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>No. Funding for this course started on 1 November 2020. Learners in 2017 paid the full fee. Today an eligible Singapore Citizen aged 40 and above pays $156 nett for the same one-day class.</p></div></details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"><summary class="mmd-faq-q">Where is the class held?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>12 Woodlands Square, #07-85/86/87 Woods Square Tower 1, Singapore 737715 &mdash; a five-minute walk from Woodlands MRT (NS9). The venue is disabled-friendly.</p></div></details> </div> <h2>Where to go next</h2> <p>Nine years on, the components have got cheaper and the libraries better, but the path in has not changed: one board, one sensor, one day where the code finally touches something real.</p> <ol> <li>Join the next <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-practical-electronics-design-with-arduino-microcontroller.html"><strong>WSQ - Practical Electronics Design with Arduino Microcontroller</strong></a> class &mdash; from <strong>$156 nett</strong> for eligible Singapore Citizens aged 40 and above.</li> <li>Then put the sensors online: our <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/blog/iot-class-2017-deepak-bukit-timah">2017 Internet of Things class</a> ran on the same idea a few months earlier, wiring sensors to the internet instead of an LCD.</li> </ol>', 'Tertiary Infotech Academy', 1, '2017-08-08', 'TGS-2020506075', 'TGS-2020506075', 'Our 2017 Arduino Electronics Class | WSQ Arduino Course Singapore', 'Inside our 8 August 2017 Arduino electronics class in Singapore: LCDs, analogue and digital sensors, servos and an ultrasonic build. The WSQ course today is funded from $156 nett.', 'arduino course singapore, wsq arduino training, practical electronics design, embedded systems singapore, arduino microcontroller class, arduino for beginners singapore, skillsfuture arduino, sensors and actuators course, arduino robotics training', 'manual-skip', NOW(), NOW() FROM DUAL WHERE @is_sg > 0 AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM (SELECT `post_id` FROM `mmd_blog_post` WHERE `url_key` = 'arduino-electronics-class-august-2017') x);


